// Syntax-highlighted code listings: \code[lang]{…} and the code environment.
//
// Render with:  texish scripts/code-demo.script -p letter

\font lmroman 12 regular

\bold{Code listings}

Source code is set with \verb|\code|, in the JetBrains Mono code face. A bare
\verb|\code{…}| sets the body plainly; a \verb|\code[language]{…}| highlights it
with that language's grammar and the current theme (\verb|\set codetheme {…}|,
a light theme by default so the ink reads on white paper).

A short JSON value, highlighted inline-style as a block:

\code[json]{{"name": "texish", "version": 5, "cross": ["jvm", "js", "native"]}}

For a whole listing — and for code whose braces do not balance — the code
environment reads everything up to its end and takes its language from
\verb|\set codelang|:

\set codelang {scala}
\begin{code}
def quadratic(a: Double, b: Double, c: Double) =
  val disc = b * b - 4 * a * c     // the discriminant
  if disc < 0 then None
  else
    val r = math.sqrt(disc)
    Some((-b + r) / (2 * a), (-b - r) / (2 * a))
\end{code}

The theme is selectable (\verb|\set codetheme {…}|); here is a Python snippet
under a different light theme:

\set codetheme {gruvbox-light}
\code[python]{def greet(name):
    print(f"hello, {name}")   # an f-string
    return len(name)}

Dark themes (monokai, dracula, nord, …) are available too, for rendering onto a
dark background. Running text resumes as an ordinary paragraph after each block.
